noi_dung:
{align:justify|"This is a story a thousand times, ten thousand times true. I present it to you not so you may marvel at its strangeness, but to prove that in this world there truly are the six realms of rebirth and the phenomenon of karmic retribution — and moreover that this happened in our own time, in Taiwan. This record was written in Mai Lieu village.}
{align:justify|Why this record was written}
{align:justify|Around February this year (the 50th year of the Republic), Master Tinh Van accepted an invitation to lecture on the sutras at Ho Vi, accompanied by Master Chu Van. With the days free, several of us lay followers went with the two masters to tour the neighboring villages near Ho Vi.}
{align:justify|Meanwhile, while Master Tinh Van lectured, Nun Tri Dao was busy at Mai Lieu, and since I had never been to Mai Lieu, I wished to visit its sights.}
{align:justify|Mai Lieu is a coastal land, its transport inconvenient, with nothing much to see. After visiting Nun Tri Dao at Tu Van Temple, we meant to return to Ho Vi — but the abbot of Tu Van Temple insisted we stay for lunch. With the bus already missed, we stayed on at the Thuong Lieu Thien hall — and it was there, prompted by Mr. Hua Ti Thach, that this strange tale came up.}
{align:justify|This story is one of borrowing a body to return to life. It happened long ago, but its owner steadfastly refused to retell it, so very few knew of it — only residents near Mai Lieu village. People elsewhere, meeting or hearing mention of it, stayed in the dark, for no one ever told them the wondrous tale; some even deemed it impossible. For it is a little-known story — even the person herself did not wish to raise it.}
{align:justify|When we first heard it, the telling was disjointed and unordered, sounding tangled and confused. Though we heard little and understood less, the words "borrowing a body to return in spirit" gripped us, and we longed to get to the bottom of it. So we hurried through lunch and rushed straight back to Ho Vi, determined to interview the story's principals at any cost.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa borrows a body to return to life}
{align:justify|The heroine of this uncanny tale now lives at house No. 95, Trung Son Street, Mai Lieu village. The house is a building-materials shop; its owner is Mr. Ngo Thu Dac, and his wife Mrs. Ngo Lam Cuong. When we arrived, the wife was out in the fields and Mr. Ngo was busy working; learning our purpose, his face showed clear displeasure. After the three of us greeted him in turn, he reluctantly told us what had happened. He said:}
{align:justify|"It happened in the 48th year of the Republic. I earn my living in construction and had joined the building works at Dao Hai Phong on Dai Tay islet. While there I seldom came home. Chancing home once, I found my wife ill — but when I went back to Dao Hai Phong, her illness eased. Later I came home more often, and my wife's illness grew ever graver, until the Dao Hai Phong works were finished and I returned for good — by which time my wife's sickness was past curing. Her illness was not so dangerous in itself, but her mind was then abnormal; she kept raving and raging. We meant to take her to a psychiatric hospital, but she refused. Even joining forces we could not seize her, and she kept shouting:}
{align:justify|'Do not take me to the asylum — I am not mad! I am from Kinmen, my name is Chu Tu Hoa!'}
{align:justify|Moreover her accent had utterly changed — but I never believed my wife's body had been occupied by another's soul.}
{align:justify|Mr. Ngo fell pensive as if recalling the past, his gaze resting on the couple's photograph, sighing softly before going on:}
{align:justify|"I truly cannot fathom how so strange a thing could happen in this world! Still less could I imagine it falling upon my own house."}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, he continued:}
{align:justify|"While I worked on the construction, every day cycling home from Dao Hai Phong, I always felt something heavy on my shoulders — but supposing it was the steep road, I paid it no mind."}
{align:justify|Only later did I learn: each time I came home, that Kinmen maiden — riding behind my bicycle — clung to me all the way home.}
{align:justify|At this the gentleman would say no more; pleading tea-making, he ended the tale.}
{align:justify|Just then, his nephew — his elder sister's son, a young man over twenty — was helping us and chatting along, when Mr. Hua, our guide, arrived, saying he would go find Mrs. Ngo for us. He told us many wished to meet her, but she refused them all. So this time, whether she would grant a meeting, he dared not guarantee. Still, he would do his utmost to find her.}
{align:justify|Mr. Ngo's nephew told us:}
{align:justify|"While my aunt was ill, I always helped my uncle nurse her. Sometimes my aunt wept, sometimes she muttered things we could not understand. Often, lying down, she would suddenly sit bolt upright; my uncle and I together pressed her back to bed — but then her strength was immense, and far from pinning her down, we were flung off instead."}
{align:justify|I thought: how could a woman be so strong? In truth it was my aunt's "friends" helping her. At this the nephew looked mysterious; I knew by "friends" he meant other spirits.}
{align:justify|He went on:}
{align:justify|"When we understood that Aunt Cuong's body had been entered by another soul, we knew not what to do. We could only let her rest and mend. At first she found everything unfamiliar. When my uncle called her A Cuong, she protested:}
{align:justify|'My name is Chu Tu Hoa, not A Cuong!'}
{align:justify|When her mother and sister came to visit, she insisted:}
{align:justify|'I do not know you people — who are you?'}
{align:justify|Of course she recognized none of our neighbors. (Here the nephew glanced warily inside, as if fearing his uncle might suddenly appear and overhear.) He lowered his voice:}
{align:justify|'Toward his family my uncle is most responsible — but he and his former wife (the old A Cuong) clashed constantly and lived unharmoniously. Yet my uncle was always upright, never seeking girlfriends or affairs outside.'}
{align:justify|But that time, at the Dao Hai Phong works, many laborers said they saw a girl keeping close beside my uncle, and they would tease:}
{align:justify|'Who would have thought Mr. Ngo was such a player!...'}
{align:justify|Once the eldest workman, resting, raised the matter of the girl beside my uncle, hinting and joking that he was a lucky man — but my uncle looked utterly baffled. He flatly denied it, saying he never brought any woman to the site. Deny as he might, the workers never stopped gossiping... My uncle supposed they were idle, teasing him for fun. So he ignored what everyone said.}
{align:justify|Little did he dream that the 'girl' everyone saw beside him then is our present aunt (Chu Tu Hoa). Truly, before entering Aunt A Cuong's body, Chu Tu Hoa followed beside my uncle every day.}
{align:justify|He lit a cigarette and went on:}
{align:justify|'Strange indeed to tell: the Dao Hai Phong works employed a great many hands, but before, every contractor who took it on lost money, and laborers at the site kept falling. But once my uncle contracted the works, not only did he earn a great deal, every worker stayed safe. Who knows — was it the spirits of Dao Hai Phong secretly sheltering and blessing us?'}
{align:justify|By now Mr. Ngo had brought out several cups of tea, and over tea we listened as his nephew continued:}
{align:justify|'You may not believe it, but this is what I lived through myself — telling it still frightens me. It went like this: when my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa) had just recovered, she often said friends came visiting and bade us prepare chairs and incense to receive them. But whenever we obediently did as she ordered, no one ever came — yet we heard her talking as if guests had arrived, chatting away merrily, laughing as she spoke. Stranger still, the bamboo chairs looked for all the world as if people were truly sitting in them, for they creaked and groaned.'}
{align:justify|Until my aunt said farewell to the guests, when the chairs sounded again, as if people were rising to leave. It was as though the unseen, fearing my aunt would be lonely, had come to visit and keep her company. But after that one time, they never came again.}
{align:justify|Since recovering, my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa) seems to know everything — she is literate, she can do anything. Compared with the old Aunt Cuong, who could not read, her bearing and conduct differ as heaven from earth — like two utterly different people.}
{align:justify|In the old days my former aunt could only cook rice and nothing else. But since falling ill and mending, she has changed completely.}
{align:justify|My present aunt not only works the fields but shoulders all the other heavy labor too. As for cooking, she says she never did it. It is all backwards and most strange. Nor is that all: every taste, gesture, shape, and step is utterly changed. Of course her voice and accent changed entirely — my aunt's speech today is pure Kinmen dialect.}
{align:justify|At this Mr. Ngo sipped his tea, looked around, and listened to our talk. His nephew pointed at the statues of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva and Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva enshrined on the altar and told us:}
{align:justify|'My uncle used to worship only his ancestors — these Buddha and Bodhisattva statues were invited home for worship by my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa). Let me tell you: my'}
{align:justify|former aunt loved eating fish and meat, but since the soul changed bodies, my new aunt not only dislikes fish and meat — put anything savory before her and she absolutely will not eat. So she always eats separately, never with the family.}
{align:justify|Just then Mr. Hua returned outside, and our eyes searched for the story's 'heroine' to see if she would come with him. He shook his head: "Alas, 'she' would not come — and wept so bitterly!"}
{align:justify|So Bao Phung and I followed layman Hua out to persuade Chu Tu Hoa to return.}
{align:justify|For our coming had quietly wounded Chu Tu Hoa's heart. When we saw her, she seemed drained of strength, leaning against a neighbor's gatepost. Eyes tightly closed, tears streaming down. I thought she must have sat here weeping a long while. Only after long comforting could we help her home.}
{align:justify|This time our visit reminded her of her Kinmen family, so her heart brimmed with unceasing sorrow. Though she strained to talk with us, after one or two words she burst into tears and could utter no sound.}
{align:justify|That day she strained to tell us in broken tones: "Her name was Chu Tu Hoa, her home in Kinmen; her father Chu Thanh Hai, her mother Thai Diep. She was 18 then. War having broken out in Kinmen, she followed the crowd aboard a fishing boat to flee. Then the boat drifted long at sea; all ran out of food and died of hunger and thirst. At last she too fainted. After none knew how long, she woke to find the boat drifted to the sea islet of Dai Tay. She was rescued alive — but afterward the fishermen pushed the boat back out to drift away"...}
{align:justify|At this she covered her face and fled inside. We longed to know more, but seeing her so stricken, we could not press her further. Time too was short — we had to catch the bus back to Ho Vi — so we all rose to bid the household farewell.}
{align:justify|Leaving, we promised that next time we came to Mai Lieu we would give Chu Tu Hoa a Buddhist rosary.}
{align:justify|Seizing gold and murdering, repaid at once}
{align:justify|Mr. Hua came with us. On the road to the bus stop he told us more:}
{align:justify|"Chu Tu Hoa in flight might well have survived. The moment the fishermen rescued her, she said: 'I beg you, save my life — whatever it takes, as bride or as servant I can serve... and all the money aboard I give to you'... But that fisherman was too wicked and utterly conscienceless! He seized the gold and flung the person into the sea... Yet in the end he could not live in peace to enjoy the ill-gotten gold. For before long his whole family died one by one! Now only one son remains — a little mad boy, dreadfully insane. Ah! Buddhism's 'karmic retribution' is mistaken not at all!"}
{align:justify|At this he looked over us all and went on:}
{align:justify|"Strange too to tell: when Chu Tu Hoa recovered, someone carried the news to Dai Tay, and Dai Tay folk hearing it were all amazed, calling it too strange. Some knew well how, years before, that mad boy's family robbed and murdered this girl — so they led the mad little boy (the robber's son) to meet Tu Hoa. To everyone's surprise, the moment they reached the gate, Chu Tu Hoa barred them from entering, weeping: 'Has your family not harmed me enough that you come to grieve me more?'"}
{align:justify|Old A Cuong had never been to Dai Tay, and when the mad boy came, no one there knew the affair in detail — yet Chu Tu Hoa recognized it all at a glance. That is truly strange.}
{align:justify|Gifting the rosary, revisiting Mai Lieu}
{align:justify|Around July this year, layman Hung Cu Minh came to Ho Vi to teach fellow devotees to chant Buddhist hymns. In conversation, Master Chu Van mentioned this 'borrowed body returning in spirit' affair. Layman Nang heard it with great interest... and since I had promised Chu Tu Hoa a rosary, I seized the chance to bring Layman Nang to Mai Lieu.}
{align:justify|Layman Nang had once lived in Kinmen, so Kinmen matters were familiar to him. On the road he told me many Kinmen tales — architecture, trades, customs, folkways, and more...}
{align:justify|These were all materials to clarify things when meeting Chu Tu Hoa.}
{align:justify|That day the weather was foul; drizzle met the bus on the road. I feared the rain would worsen — but when the bus reached Mai Lieu the rain had stopped. I could not help rejoicing, silently reciting in my heart: "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!"}
{align:justify|All Kinmen matters remembered}
{align:justify|With rain, Chu Tu Hoa had not gone to the fields, and learning she was home, my heart lifted as of a burden dropped.}
{align:justify|Perhaps because I brought several people, Chu Tu Hoa hesitated long before coming out. But this time she seemed far calmer; seeing us, she smiled and nodded greeting — though the smile was strained.}
{align:justify|First I gave her the rosary, then chatted idly. With last time's lesson, I did not broach the matter directly but circled round it, beginning with religion.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa said:}
{align:justify|"As a child I deeply believed in the Buddha — I even ate lifelong vegetarian. Busy as work is now, morning and evening I worship the Buddha. I know well: the Buddha's words are never wrong. Each person should do good and never do evil — for evil reaps no good fruit!"}
{align:justify|Last visit I had heard neighbors say Chu Tu Hoa worshipped the Buddha diligently every day, and I thought: "Surely this is why she could return in spirit to the human world!" So I seized the chance to ask:}
{align:justify|"You say you believed deeply as a child — is there a Buddha Recitation Hall in Kinmen?"}
{align:justify|She thought a moment, then answered:}
{align:justify|"I do not know. But in my house we worshipped the Buddha and Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva; I worshipped only at home — my whole family worshipped the Buddha."}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Do you still remember Kinmen things now?"}
{align:justify|She sighed:}
{align:justify|"Alas! I remember! All of it! But it is past — why raise it?"}
{align:justify|"If someone now wished to help you find your parents, would you consent?"}
{align:justify|"Of course, I would rejoice. But who would help me? And even found, I fear my kin would not know me!" — she smiled bitterly and went on: "For the body I wear now is not the form I wore leaving Kinmen!"}
{align:justify|At this she pressed her lips tight and her eyes reddened, but she strained not to weep before guests.}
{align:justify|I pointed to Layman Nang seated below and said:}
{align:justify|"This gentleman lived long in Kinmen; moreover he believes in the Buddha and knows Kinmen well. Tell him of drifting at sea, and relate it truthfully. Afterward..."}
{align:justify|At this two round tears like pearls appeared in her eyes, and she hastily wiped them away.}
{align:justify|I interrupted, continuing for her:}
{align:justify|"Afterward they seized your money and flung you into the sea — so their whole family later died out, leaving only a mad little boy..."}
{align:justify|Before I could finish, she cut in:}
{align:justify|"Ah! You have heard this too?! Truly, the gold aboard was not all mine, but belonged to many fellow refugees. And that they seized the gold and then their whole family died out is true. But I believe in the Buddha: though I know them ruthless and conscienceless, I nurse no feud with them. This reckoning comes from my fellow voyagers' indignant dead, avenging the wrong!"}
{align:justify|I asked her again:}
{align:justify|"Before coming to Mr. Ngo's house, where did you dwell?"}
{align:justify|The question displeased her somewhat, but at last she answered:}
{align:justify|"I dwelt at Dao Hai Phong village on Dai Tay, where green trees abound and the sea is most beautiful. I lived there many years."}
{align:justify|"Did you like the place?"}
{align:justify|"I liked it; I stayed there long. Cast into the sea, my soul lingered round Hai Phong islet. I stayed there some ten days before Lord Vuong took me as his follower. Lord Vuong told me my lifespan was not yet ended — I might borrow the body of Ngo Thu Dac's wife to return to life. He bade me lodge meanwhile at the Bac Prince Temple. Before long Ngo Thu Dac came to Dao Hai Phong for work, and I followed Ngo Thu Dac..."}
{align:justify|When Ngo Thu Dac finished his work and returned home, I followed home too, to await my chance. Days later Mrs. Lam Cuong Yeu's illness worsened, her soul departing to another realm. I seized the chance to borrow the body and return in spirit.}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, she went on:}
{align:justify|"But borrowing a body is no easy thing. Entering another's frame is most anguishing work. Thanks to Lord Vuong's aid, it took over twenty days to complete."}
{align:justify|"How did it feel then?"}
{align:justify|"Most natural, yet unfamiliar" — meaning before she had been a maiden, and now must wear the body of a married elder woman.}
{align:justify|I changed the subject:}
{align:justify|"Is Mai Lieu as fine as Kinmen?"}
{align:justify|"Mai Lieu? How could this place compare with Kinmen? Kinmen houses are all built of brick, its roads flat and straight. Where we lived was a merchants' quarter — bustling and lively. But Mai Lieu houses are built with no order, all jumbled and chaotic!"}
{align:justify|Layman Nang agreed with her.}
{align:justify|By Layman Nang's reckoning, Chu Tu Hoa fled in the 43rd year of the Republic, for in that year many saw the army shipping guns; he believed Thang Nhan would take over well! Of course I too urged Thang Nhan to obey his father. Dwelling in their house, I have always hoped the family might live together in harmony and peace.}
{align:justify|Speaking of the children, her cheeks suddenly flushed. (Naturally — reckoning by her present age, she is still so young, yet suddenly an older youth calls her mother; she must feel most unaccustomed!)}
{align:justify|Taking a keepsake photograph}
{align:justify|Before we knew it we had talked several hours; time to say farewell. I rose, took her hand, and comforted her:}
{align:justify|"All have treated you well, so you too should release and forget. Buddhism says all comes from gathered causes and conditions. Perhaps you and the Ngo family share affinity, that from distant Kinmen you drifted here to live among them."}
{align:justify|She nodded. I went on:}
{align:justify|"In any case, every day you sincerely recite the Buddha's name — wherever you live, practice just so. No need to reach a Recitation Hall. Buddhas and Bodhisattvas ever bless and shelter you! Moreover in the Buddhist spirit, benefit others first, self after. You have helped the whole household and made them joyful — this too is great merit!"}
{align:justify|She stayed silent, and I added:}
{align:justify|"If you wish to visit a Buddhist hall, when I am free I shall take you to Ho Vi for a visit. I hope from now you will be at peace and no longer often feel unbearable sorrow!"}
{align:justify|She grasped my hand, moved, thanking me again and again.}
{align:justify|Before leaving, I invited her to photograph with me as a keepsake. She seemed reluctant, till Thang Nhan coaxed and urged her long, and she nodded assent.}
{align:justify|As we left, Thang Nhan saw us off. On the road I asked the boy about his mother. He said:}
{align:justify|"My mother grew up in Mai Lieu from childhood and never went to Dai Tay or Kinmen. After she recovered she utterly changed into another person. I truly cannot believe such things. But the body is still my mother's — yet Mother steadfastly refuses to admit she is A Cuong. Kinfolk visiting — even Grandmother and the aunts — Mother recognizes none."}
{align:justify|The whole affair amazes the family. In my heart I feel somewhat wronged. I truly do not know what to call her.}
{align:justify|At this the boy paused. I asked:}
{align:justify|"And... now?"}
{align:justify|The boy smiled sadly:}
{align:justify|"Of course I still call her Mother — for it is my mother's body!"}
{align:justify|"Do you believe the 'borrowing a body to return in spirit'?"}
{align:justify|"Once I utterly disbelieved. My mother never went to Hai Phong islet, yet my present mother often describes Hai Phong islet. Moreover in the 48th year of the Republic I joined a camp in the Philippines with a friend from Kinmen, whose accent was most distinct."}
{align:justify|When I came home Mother was ill; once mended, her speech utterly changed — her accent exactly like that Kinmen friend's. Moreover she can tell a great many Kinmen tales. So I believe my later mother truly is a Kinmen person.}
{align:justify|Journalist Ly Ngoc's conclusion:}
{align:justify|I tell you readers this tale with no wish to stir idle curiosity to seek out Chu Tu Hoa. I only use this story to prove that Buddhism's "rebirth through the six realms, karmic retribution" is exact, and this principle ever holds.}
{align:justify|Lastly, here, I hope we shall all bless Chu Tu Hoa.}
{align:justify|Commentary:}
{align:justify|Every day we study the Buddha's teaching, ever reminded by Him: "This body is unreal, fragile, able to fall apart at any moment"... Yet we can hardly penetrate it, for from the moment we open our eyes at birth we already wear this body. But Chu Tu Hoa, in the very instant of "borrowing a body to return to life," returning to live, sees most easily that the body she wears is NOT hers.}
{align:justify|On the refugee road, being robbed and cast into the sea might be seen as old karmic debt, or a new cause the robber began to sow. But Chu Tu Hoa confided: "She understands the Buddha's teaching, believes in cause and effect, and bears no hatred toward her murderers — and when the robber's whole family died out, it was the reckoning of the indignant dead who voyaged with her."}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa is a forgiving girl of high responsibility. Only an eighteen-year-old spirit, borrowing the body of a woman far older, with a son her own age — upon reviving, she must shoulder so much work: out to the fields at dawn, watching the paddies' water by night. Her husband's family runs construction and sells building materials besides. Where the text says "heavy hauling work, demanding strength, she does it all, shouldering everything" — suddenly I ache and pity her.}
{align:justify|Before, the former wife did but one thing: cook rice. But she, revived, embraces too much, shoulders too much, though none compels her. This is called high responsibility: living considerately, repaying kindness with kindness — for borrowing another's body, she must work through that body to repay the debt.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa's awakening is surely stronger than ours, for she has lived through life and death, through returning in spirit, and through living in a "borrowed" body in the truest sense.}
{align:justify|I have checked: she fled in the 43rd year of the Republic, that is 1954, and died that year. But only in the 48th year of the Republic, 1959, did she "borrow a body to return to life." So she spent five years dwelling in the yin realm. She says in those five years she stayed at Dao Hai Phong, its scenery lovely, its trees fair, and she liked it there very much. I believe she did not fall among ghosts, for her heart harbored no anger or hatred — her heart was most kind, and realms ever match the heart. So though in the yin realm, she describes it leisurely, as one content.}
{align:justify|Until the spirits told her that Mrs. A Duong was ill, her lifespan nearly spent, and she might "borrow the body to return to life" — only then did she seek out Mr. Ngo, "ride" along, and hover round him, waiting for him to go home so she might follow and enter the body.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa had to enter a body twice, half again her own age — unwed, yet made wife and mother. Revived, never again to meet the father and mother her heart remembered (parting in life hurts worse than parting in death), she must shoulder a new family in poorer estate. Yet she still fulfills every duty and obligation.}
{align:justify|After returning in spirit, Chu Tu Hoa once had the family light incense and set chairs to talk with her friends. These friends may be spirits of the yin realm, fellow dead who perished with her at sea — and guardian deities too... But fine it is that they came to comfort her only once after her borrowing, then never again. Meaning the two realms are sharply divided; let each live in peace in their own realm. This too is the finest mutual shelter.}
{align:justify|Eighteen years old, yet Chu Tu Hoa's experience exceeds ours by far — for she has been human and ghost, lived through theyin realm, through living in a "borrowed" body in the truest sense.}
{align:justify|All this has kindled the lamp of awakening high in her, so revived, she hastens to invite Buddha and Bodhisattva statues for worship, morning and evening diligent in obeisance without cease, steadfastly vegetarian, longing to ascend a temple Recitation Hall. All this I understand. And surely readers too share fellow feeling with her, each in their own way.}
{align:justify|I translate this piece not to amuse readers, but that we may share more fine things of transmigration's teaching, of the realms... and understand the Buddha's words never deceive.}
{align:justify|Journalist Ly Ngoc once hoped we would bless Chu Tu Hoa after reading this tale. I guess Chu Tu Hoa must be old now, of the "seventy years rare from olden times" age (?) — and none knows whether she lives or has passed?}
{align:justify|For myself, before closing this book, I would pray blessings... not for Chu Tu Hoa alone, but for all readers — wishing you all everlasting peace, ever knowing how to live so that, in yin realm or bright, you are happy forever.}
{align:justify|Translated 29/3/2013}
{align:justify|Hanh Doan}
{align:justify|NOTES}
{align:justify|Correctly translated means "bestowed."}
{align:justify|Real names have been changed.}
{align:justify|Married women in China are named in the style with the husband's surname first — Mr. Ngo's wife was born Lam.}
